# pcrec-bench — line endings, and why this file exists.
#
# A RECORD's content hash covers its row lines AS WRITTEN
# (docs/design/record_schema.md §3): line 1 is canonicalised before
# hashing, but every result row is hashed byte for byte, trailing
# whitespace stripped and nothing else. That makes the hash a real
# tamper check and it makes the file format fragile in exactly one
# direction: anything that rewrites the bytes on the way in or out of
# git — `core.autocrlf`, a CRLF checkout on Windows, an editor's
# "normalize line endings on save" — changes the hash of a file nobody
# edited, and it presents as rule X6, "edited, truncated or restamped?".
#
# The fix is to tell git these are binary as far as EOL conversion is
# concerned. `-text` disables all line-ending normalization; it does NOT
# make the files binary for diff purposes, so `git diff` on a record is
# still readable.
#
# Records are never edited (requirements.md §6), so there is no case in
# which normalizing one is the right answer.

*.jsonl            -text
store/**/*.jsonl   -text
